1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:11,000
 In the handbook the chapter on project financing also includes non-economic attributes but we already covered that when we talked about cost benefit analysis.

2
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:15,000
 Let's have a look at two things.

3
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:18,000
 The cost of capital and the MAR.

4
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:22,000
 We will not go into detail on debt or equity at all.

5
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:30,000
 Not because they are not important, it's very interesting but to my feeling it's a bit out of scope of our engineering economy course.

6
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:36,000
 So we already met the cost of capital.

7
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:43,000
 The cost of capital is the weighted average interest rate based on debt and equity sources.

8
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:46,000
 So the WAC, the weighted cost of capital.

9
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:52,000
 And debt capital is the capital that we borrow outside the company.

10
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:56,000
 Equity is our own funds and returned earnings.

11
00:00:56,000 --> 00:01:01,000
 We already discussed the MAR, minimum deductive rate of return.

12
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:07,000
 And we pointed out that that MAR takes into account the cost of capital.

13
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:10,000
 It should definitely be higher.

14
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:15,000
 In the example that you see there, the cost of capital is 8%.

15
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:18,000
 Then we definitely expect more.

16
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:26,000
 We will not be happy if the return of a project would be lower than that.

17
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:32,000
 So this is a very important slide.

18
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:35,000
 To the right you see again the weighted average cost of capital.

19
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:37,000
 And then to the left our MAR.

20
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:41,000
 We already spoke about the MAR a few times.

21
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:48,000
 We pointed out that the MAR cannot be found somewhere in literature or in the internet or in handbooks.

22
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:52,000
 But that the MAR is something that the company is deciding for themselves.

23
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:55,000
 And now what are the factors affecting the MAR?

24
00:01:55,000 --> 00:01:57,000
 Project risks.

25
00:01:57,000 --> 00:02:01,000
 The higher the perceived risk, the higher the MAR will be set.

26
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:03,000
 Investment opportunities.

27
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:10,000
 If a company sees investment opportunities, maybe market opportunities, they could lower the MAR temporarily.

28
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:15,000
 Government interventions can cause companies to raise or lower the MAR.

29
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:19,000
 Government interventions like tariffs, subsidies, etc.

30
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:22,000
 Also the tax structure has an impact.

31
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:28,000
 If corporate tax rates are rising, then also the MAR will be higher.

32
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:30,000
 Limited capital.

33
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:34,000
 As we have less and less capital, the MAR will increase.

34
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:37,000
 And also competition.

35
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:42,000
 Stronger or not competition at all.

36
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:48,000
 Or lowered competition can cause companies to raise or lower the MAR.

37
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:53,000
 So these are a few important factors that are affecting the MAR.

38
00:02:53,000 --> 00:02:57,000
 And I expect you to know about them.

39
00:02:57,000 --> 00:02:59,000
 An example of a WAC calculation.

40
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:01,000
 Weight at average cost of capital.

41
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:04,000
 We already saw a similar example earlier on in the course.

42
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:12,000
 So here we are having a case study about a company that is specializing in producing cold weather clothing and accessories.

43
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:17,000
 And it's expanding its ski jacket and wood stock manufacturing facilities.

44
00:03:17,000 --> 00:03:20,000
 It plans to borrow 2.5 million at 7% interest.

45
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:25,000
 Issue stock worth of 4 million worth at 5.9%.

46
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:31,000
 And use 1.5 million of retained earnings at 5.1% to finance the project.

47
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:35,000
 Then the question is, determine the company's WAC.

48
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:42,000
 Well we know that the total project cost is 4 plus 1.5 plus 2.5 is 8 million.

49
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:48,000
 Then if we see the 5.9% will be used for 4 million.

50
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:50,000
 So that's half of it.

51
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:56,000
 So 4 divided by 8 times 5.9 is our first term.

52
00:03:56,000 --> 00:04:03,000
 Then 1.5 over 8 we have at the 5.1%.

53
00:04:03,000 --> 00:04:09,000
 And then the remaining 2.5 of the 8 will be financed at 7%.

54
00:04:09,000 --> 00:04:13,000
 So that brings our WAC to 6.09.

55
00:04:13,000 --> 00:04:20,000
 We will not talk about debt capital and equity capital as I consider it.

56
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:22,000
 As I already mentioned.

57
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:32,000
 To be outside of the scope maybe just one word of advice that a good balance between debt and equity funding is to be recommended.

